<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Qaraqush's Tripolitania</h1><p class="st-meta">1172 CE – 1212 CE (approx.) · capital Tripoli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The freebooting domain of Sharaf al-Din Qaraqush, an Ayyubid mamluk who carved out power in Tripolitania and southern Ifriqiya against the Almohads.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharaf-al-din-qaraqush">Qaraqush</a>, an Armenian mamluk in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taqi-al-din-umar">Taqi al-Din</a>, nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, led Turkish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Ghuzz</a> soldiers west from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> around 1172. Exploiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> weakness east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, he gathered support from Arab tribes and took towns in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tripolitania">Tripolitania</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafusa-mountains">Jabal Nafusa</a> and the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabes">Gabès</a>, acting as an adventurer more than as an agent of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>His power rested on a mobile army of Turkish soldiers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> allies rather than settled institutions. He allied at times with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banu-ghaniya-of-ifriqiya">Banu Ghaniya of Ifriqiya</a> against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, and the two sides' campaigns ravaged southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tripolitania">Tripolitania</a>. Little is known of any administration beyond tribute and plunder.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharaf-al-din-qaraqush">Qaraqush</a> lost ground to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> counter-attacks and then quarrelled with his <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banu-ghaniya-of-ifriqiya">Banu Ghaniya</a> allies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ghaniya">Yahya ibn Ghaniya</a> captured and killed him around 1212 in the oases of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-fezzan">Fezzan</a> region, and his followers dispersed.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/qaraqushs-tripolitania.jpg" alt="Qaraqush's Tripolitania" width="320" height="231"><figcaption>Image: naturalearthdata.com, offered to the Public Domain per Terms of Use Drawing: पाटलिपुत्र (talk) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayyubid_expansion_1174-1193.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1172 CE – 1212 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tripoli</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sharaf al-Din Qaraqush</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>, <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sharaf al-Din Qaraqush</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1192&amp;focus=qaraqushs-tripolitania" aria-label="Qaraqush's Tripolitania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5018 983 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Qaraqush's Tripolitania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5338 1239L5811 1337M5338 1239L4794 1282"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5811 1337h0M4794 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5338 1239h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tripoli. <a href="/atlas?year=1192&amp;focus=qaraqushs-tripolitania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
